WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.830
- Welcome everyone to a new coffee break,

00:00:01.830 --> 00:00:03.660
here on my channel Firm Learning.

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:06.060
My name is Heinrich, I'm a
former McKinsey consultant.

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:09.690
Today, I will talk about one
of the most common mistakes

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:12.150
that young leaders, young professionals,

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:15.810
people working in consulting,
also in industry are doing.

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:17.730
It's a mistake that many people confuse

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:20.280
with having been the victim
of corporate politics.

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:22.230
But no, if this happens to you,

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:24.510
it's not due to politics, but it's on you.

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:26.340
It's a mistake that you made.

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:27.630
And trust, it is going to be juicy

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:29.070
and extremely relevant

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:30.810
for every young professional out there.

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:33.360
So in this fictive scenario,
there was a team lead

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:35.400
who worked on an important new initiative

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:36.780
over the last weeks.

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:38.850
Now there's this meeting
where this person's presenting

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:40.980
the progress and also the way forward

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:42.360
on his or her initiatives

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:44.910
to the senior leaders of the organization.

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:45.990
But now what happens?

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:48.900
These senior leaders actually
criticize the approach.

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:51.120
They do not agree with
the proposed action plans.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:52.860
They do not agree with
what is being presented

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:54.510
to them at this point in time.

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:56.910
And indeed, due to this, the
whole meeting is derailed,

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:58.860
and, of course, also the reputation

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:01.230
of the person presenting this will suffer.

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:03.600
Their standing will take a hit.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:05.520
So does this sound familiar?

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:07.500
Think about it, what went wrong?

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:10.680
Could the person have done
something to prevent this?

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:12.960
And I want really to
literally stop the video now,

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:15.270
just read through this
and reflect on that.

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:17.040
What is your opinion on this?

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:20.580
Could this person have
done something differently

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:24.213
to shape this meeting in a way
to lead to a better outcome?

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:26.970
So let's get into it.

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:28.620
What could this person have done?

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:30.720
And to better understand
that, I first want to look

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:33.180
into the typical process
that a typical team lead

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:35.490
in such a situation
will likely go through.

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:38.310
And indeed, this process
consists of three steps.

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:40.620
So first, that person
will conduct analysis

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:43.680
and derive results often
together with the team

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:45.900
to then get to something
that can be presented.

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:48.000
And indeed, this is then the second step.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:51.477
Prepare a presentation, prepare
slides, prepare a document.

00:01:51.477 --> 00:01:52.830
And then of course, in the end,

00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:55.080
there's the presentation itself,

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:56.850
where the pages are being presented

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:59.280
and are being discussed in the meeting.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:00.840
So let's go here step by step.

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:02.430
Step number one, what happened here?

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:04.800
Well, of course, often
information has been gathered

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:08.130
by colleagues, other sources,
maybe some expert interviews

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:10.140
that this person will have conducted.

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:11.580
You do this together with the team,

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:13.680
and then you derive the results together

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:16.050
with your colleagues
on the topics at hand.

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:18.990
Second step, where you
structure then all the analysis

00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:21.000
into some type of presentation document.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.340
Of course, hopefully, you apply the good

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:26.310
slide writing principles, the
top communication techniques,

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:28.530
top-down communication,
per every principle

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:31.200
that we talk about so
frequently here on this channel.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:34.230
If all of this says nothing
to you, I will link a playlist

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:37.020
above here where you will read
about more of these topics,

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:38.610
watch videos on exactly this.

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:40.230
Last, but not least,
there's a presentation.

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:42.450
Of course, often the
team lead will deliver

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:45.150
this presentation him or herself

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:48.390
in front of the senior
leadership of the organization.

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:49.223
And now, once again,

00:02:49.223 --> 00:02:51.630
I want you to pause the
video and think about that.

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:53.430
Thinking about this approach,

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:55.050
will this likely be successful?

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:57.783
Will this likely lead to a good result?

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:02.400
In my humble opinion, in my experiences,

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:03.930
no, this will not work, right,

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:05.430
because what is the problem?

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:08.190
If you do this, this process often leads

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:09.660
to negative outcomes

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:12.150
as also shared in the
initial example, why?

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:16.470
Because the team lead did not
manage to align the results

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:19.440
with all the key stakeholders
of the organization.

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:23.220
Alignment, one of the
most important topics

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:26.010
in every big corporate,
in consulting for sure.

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:29.520
It is a topic so common,
so important in consulting

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:31.800
that it is literally a meme.

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:35.017
Here my friends @ConsultingHumor
prepared this little quote,

00:03:35.017 --> 00:03:38.010
"Just had a meeting to
align on how we will align

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:41.280
with the client during our
weekly alignment meeting."

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:44.250
Right, alignment, alignment,
alignment, alignment.

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:46.920
You will have lots of meetings every week,

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:48.390
just to align content.

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:49.440
By the way, Consulting Humor,

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:51.690
really a great channel
for humor like this.

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:53.940
I will link the socials below
in the video description.

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:56.100
Check it out, great
content, I can promise you.

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:58.500
But why is it so important
to align content,

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:00.360
to align documents before meetings?

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:03.330
Let's go over some typical
problems that arise

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:06.000
if you do not do this,
if alignment is missing.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:08.520
Well first, of course,
colleagues will likely not have

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:10.710
any buy-in in the
solution that you present

00:04:10.710 --> 00:04:13.620
as they were not involved
in the solution process.

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:16.290
Next point, valid concerns were not heard

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:18.000
and reflected in the solution.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.400
Maybe there were really
some important things

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:23.430
that the senior leaders of
your organization were aware of

00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:24.900
that you just couldn't be aware of.

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:26.160
But of course, now you don't know

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:27.480
because you didn't involve them.

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:30.690
You didn't align your solutions
to your contents with them.

00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:34.890
Next, there's the natural
tendency to defend the status quo

00:04:34.890 --> 00:04:37.470
and think about the reasons
why proposed change is bad.

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:39.090
If you just propose something new,

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:41.100
something that will change the status quo,

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:43.410
and someone just hears
it for the first time,

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:45.667
likely their very first reaction will be,

00:04:45.667 --> 00:04:47.100
"Ah, nah, probably not.

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:48.300
Probably we don't need to do that."

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:50.130
Next point, very much related to that.

00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:52.620
No one likes to be surprised,

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:54.750
especially not in front
of their colleagues.

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:57.030
If you walk into meeting and
suddenly you hear something,

00:04:57.030 --> 00:04:59.850
see something that is
very surprising to you,

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:01.770
that you did not expect at all.

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:03.360
Ah, that's likely not great.

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:05.820
And especially if then your
other senior colleagues

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:08.040
are sitting next to you because
this also just makes you

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:11.040
look uninformed, unaware
of what is going on

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:13.530
in your organization,
especially if this was

00:05:13.530 --> 00:05:16.320
your direct superior and
that person didn't even know

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:18.870
what you were thinking about,
what you were working on.

00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:20.310
That's just bad because this also

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:22.050
makes that person look bad.

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:24.480
Next point, it's also just very generally

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:26.040
considered unprofessional.

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:29.010
You will be expected that you
will have aligned key results

00:05:29.010 --> 00:05:31.680
with all the key stakeholders,
even if maybe the people

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:33.540
did not directly task you to do that.

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:36.270
That's just an expectation
towards the role

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:38.100
of a team lead in such an organization.

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:39.150
And as I said in the beginning,

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:42.367
many people confused these types
of reactions with politics.

00:05:42.367 --> 00:05:43.920
"Oh, that person didn't like me.

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:46.020
That person had something against me.

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:48.210
That person didn't want me to succeed."

00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:49.410
Right, and pretty much blame it

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:50.910
on the culture of the organization.

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:52.830
This is not what's going on.

00:05:52.830 --> 00:05:55.050
You are hiding yourself from the truth.

00:05:55.050 --> 00:05:56.670
It was your responsibility.

00:05:56.670 --> 00:06:00.120
It is your responsibility to
make sure that these types

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:02.370
of alignment errors don't happen.

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:04.890
But let's now discuss what
this team lead could have done

00:06:04.890 --> 00:06:08.070
to properly align the results,
the contents of the work

00:06:08.070 --> 00:06:09.930
with the senior leaders,
with the stakeholders.

00:06:09.930 --> 00:06:12.990
How could the person
have improved the outcome

00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:14.250
of this important meeting?

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:15.630
And by the way, before we do it,

00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:18.420
if you took any value out
of this video at all so far,

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:20.760
please hit the Like button
for the YouTube algorithm.

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:23.490
Of course, also subscribe,
turn on the notification bell

00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:25.410
to stay up to date with all of my content.

00:06:25.410 --> 00:06:27.510
This is very, very much appreciated.

00:06:27.510 --> 00:06:29.310
But let's look at it step by step.

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:31.440
For all the three steps
you initially outline

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:34.080
and start with the first
one, to conduct an analysis

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:35.280
and derive results.

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:37.560
And here the mistake that many people do

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:40.770
is that they see their
colleagues, also their clients,

00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:42.720
just as some data sources.

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:44.550
If you just walk around the organization,

00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:46.800
just request information from people,

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:49.380
then for you to do the
analysis and to do the work,

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:51.690
well of course, you do
need all this information.

00:06:51.690 --> 00:06:55.410
This is not to be confused
with properly co-creating

00:06:55.410 --> 00:06:57.540
solutions with the relevant colleagues.

00:06:57.540 --> 00:06:59.010
So if you're leading such an initiative,

00:06:59.010 --> 00:07:01.320
very clearly think about
what the people are

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:02.880
that you want the buy-in from.

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:05.130
Think about some
appropriate workshop formats

00:07:05.130 --> 00:07:07.290
or other good ways to
directly involve them

00:07:07.290 --> 00:07:10.020
also in the process to make
sure that they feel like

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:12.180
they have been part of the solution,

00:07:12.180 --> 00:07:14.940
that they feel like it
was maybe even their idea

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:16.380
what you proposed in the end.

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:17.940
This often goes a long way.

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:21.150
Next, never, never,
never walk into a meeting

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:24.780
with your senior leaders and
letting them see the document

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:25.613
the first time.

00:07:25.613 --> 00:07:28.620
But what I mean is that you
properly take the time to,

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:31.980
in the one or two weeks before
this very important meeting,

00:07:31.980 --> 00:07:35.310
you meet with all these
people, one on one,

00:07:35.310 --> 00:07:37.620
and you guide them through the document.

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:40.260
If it's not the final document
because it's not ready yet,

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:42.660
then at least the rough
outline, the key results

00:07:42.660 --> 00:07:45.210
that you want to share and you
ask them for their feedback,

00:07:45.210 --> 00:07:47.220
you ask them for their guidance.

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:49.260
And this is, of course,
especially important

00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:51.450
also in consulting and
potentially especially challenging

00:07:51.450 --> 00:07:54.180
in consulting because then
not only you need to align

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:55.890
the documents with your clients,

00:07:55.890 --> 00:07:58.830
but you also need to align
all the key documents

00:07:58.830 --> 00:08:00.540
with your internal leadership.

00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:01.710
And this can be quite challenging

00:08:01.710 --> 00:08:03.960
because often you end up
going in circles, right?

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:06.480
So you create a document,
align it with a client.

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:09.210
Then you take the document,
show it to your partner

00:08:09.210 --> 00:08:10.620
and actually your partner disagrees.

00:08:10.620 --> 00:08:12.450
The partner think, "No, this
is not what you can do."

00:08:12.450 --> 00:08:13.980
So you turn around the document,

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:16.260
then you go back with this
document to the client,

00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:18.120
and then the client says,
"No, I disagree", right?

00:08:18.120 --> 00:08:19.830
And then this can go really in circle.

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:22.770
So that's often quite a pain,
especially as a project lead

00:08:22.770 --> 00:08:23.790
to moderate all this.

00:08:23.790 --> 00:08:24.840
And then last, but not least,

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:27.720
for this presentation in
front of the senior leaders,

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:29.340
very carefully think about whether

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:31.440
you are really the best
person to present it.

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:32.917
And of course, I understand
that maybe you'll think,

00:08:32.917 --> 00:08:35.400
"Oh, it would be great for
myself to get this visibility,

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:37.170
get this spotlight,
present in front of all

00:08:37.170 --> 00:08:38.250
these senior leaders."

00:08:38.250 --> 00:08:40.470
But sometimes, it might make more sense

00:08:40.470 --> 00:08:42.813
for potentially a person more
senior than you to present it.

00:08:42.813 --> 00:08:45.030
This is actually even more
important than in-professional

00:08:45.030 --> 00:08:48.570
services where you often
usually try that the clients

00:08:48.570 --> 00:08:50.580
are actually presenting the results

00:08:50.580 --> 00:08:52.560
of your project, of your work.

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:54.750
It's always so much more convincing, too.

00:08:54.750 --> 00:08:57.570
For instance, the board of
management of a large company

00:08:57.570 --> 00:08:59.400
to see that the results
are not being presented

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:03.390
by external consultants, but
internally by the team leads

00:09:03.390 --> 00:09:05.610
of their own organization,
because this then

00:09:05.610 --> 00:09:07.620
also to the board shows that this is not

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:09.150
just the crazy consulting idea,

00:09:09.150 --> 00:09:11.730
which afterwards people are
just throwing in the bin,

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:14.220
but this is something that
their own organization

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:15.540
really buys into it.

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:17.280
But now let me know
about your experiences,

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:19.830
any crazy anecdotes you
can share on aligning

00:09:19.830 --> 00:09:22.290
or actually not aligning
things in the proper way.

00:09:22.290 --> 00:09:23.220
Let me know in the comments.

00:09:23.220 --> 00:09:25.740
Also, if you have any other
tips on these types of topics.

00:09:25.740 --> 00:09:27.870
Of course, as always big, big thanks

00:09:27.870 --> 00:09:29.340
to all the members of the channel.

